- Annual operational carbon-intensity figures (src/datasets/countries.csv)
  are derived from the "Our World in Data" energy dataset
  (https://github.com/owid/energy-data), field `carbon_intensity_elec`, which
  compiles Ember and the Energy Institute Statistical Review of World Energy.
  Licensed under Creative Commons BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).

- The per-country real-time data-source catalogue (src/datasets/sources.csv)
  is compiled from factual information (operator names and reference URLs); the
  Electricity Maps community's electricitymaps-contrib DATA_SOURCES.md
  (https://github.com/electricitymaps/electricitymaps-contrib) was used to
  cross-check coverage, and is credited here.

- Real-time generation data is obtained from the operators and platforms named
  in each response's `data_source`. The carbon-intensity figures published here
  are COMPUTED BY THIS PROJECT from that generation data using the emission
  factors in src/factors.js; they are not published by, endorsed by, or
  attributable to those operators. In particular:

  * U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA) — accessed via the EIA API
    under its Terms of Service. EIA is identified as the source of the
    underlying generation data only. Nothing here implies EIA endorsement of
    this project, and the EIA logo is not used.

  * ENTSO-E Transparency Platform — data listed as open for re-use is available
    under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence
    (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Attribution: © ENTSO-E
    Transparency Platform. The figures here are an adaptation: hourly
    generation per production type is weighted by emission factors to derive an
    intensity, so the values differ from anything ENTSO-E publishes.

- Fuel-code mappings (src/factors.js) are derived from each data source's own
  published taxonomy — ENTSO-E PSR codes, EIA fuel-type codes, OpenElectricity
  fueltechs, Eskom's column glossary, and the Spanish/Portuguese fuel names used
  by CENACE/ONS — so the computed values are independent.

- Lifecycle emission factors follow IPCC AR6, WG III (2022), Annex III.

- Real-time readings, when enabled, are fetched from the respective grid
  operators / market data providers named in each country's `data_source`
  (e.g. ENTSO-E, EIA, NESO/Elexon, ONS, OpenNEM/OpenElectricity, EMC Singapore,
  Eskom). Those data remain subject to their publishers' own terms.
